insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-campaign', 1, 'A data manager runs a campaign over a delivery of six LAS files. What is the campaign for?', '["To find which files differ from the rest without opening each one","To decide, for each file it finds a problem in, whether that file should be flagged or dropped before it reaches the registry","To remove the need to open any file at all, since the aggregates carry everything a reader needs","To convert the whole delivery into the project''s units in one pass"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The campaign exists to locate the files that differ from the rest without a reader having to open each one. Deciding what to do about a finding stays with the person reading, and treating the campaign as the decision maker is the classic error in the second option.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-campaign', 2, 'Reading a delivery one file at a time, why is a reader likely to miss that only one file is denominated in feet?', '["The depth unit is absent from a LAS header, so it can only be recovered by comparing several files in the delivery against each other","A single-file panel does not report the depth unit","That fact is a comparison across files, so it cannot be seen while looking at any one file and has to be carried in memory instead","The conversion is applied before the reader sees the file, so the native unit is gone by then"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Being the only file in foreign units is a property of the set rather than of the file, so it is invisible on any single file and survives only in the reader''s memory of the other five. The depth unit is declared in the header of every one of these files, which is what makes the first option wrong.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-campaign', 3, 'The six files in the delivery share a sender, a field and a format. What do they not share?', '["Their curve suite, which differs from file to file across this delivery","Their quality","Their depth sample count, which the campaign has to equalise before it can aggregate anything","Their null flag declaration, which a delivery is required to keep the same in every file"]'::jsonb, 1, 'One file arrives in foreign depth units, one has no uniform depth step and one carries a dead curve, so quality is exactly what varies. The curve suite is constant across these six at 4 curves each with the index excluded, which is why the first option is wrong.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-campaign', 4, 'The campaign reports 1 dead curve in the delivery. What has the campaign not done?', '["It has not read the file''s null declaration","It has not counted that curve among the curves imported across the campaign","It has not run the completeness test on the other five files","It has not decided whether that curve should be flagged to preserve the delivery record or dropped to keep the registry honest, and it has not replaced the work of opening the file it points at"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A campaign chooses the file worth your attention and stops there, leaving the flag or drop decision and the detailed work to the reader. The second option is the classic error of assuming a curve that is dead was left out of the count of 24, when a delivered curve is imported and counted whatever is in it.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-campaign', 5, 'Which graded reading points at irregular_20.las?', '["The count of files with a uniform depth step, which is 5 of the 6 because irregular_20.las is the file that has none","The count of dead curves, which is 1","The count of curves imported across the campaign, which is 24","The count of files needing a depth unit conversion, which is 1 because exactly one file in this delivery arrived denominated in feet"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Five of the six files carry a uniform depth step and irregular_20.las is the exception that makes the reading 5 rather than 6. The conversion count of 1 points at feet_20.las and the dead curve count of 1 points at nullheavy_20.las.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-campaign', 6, 'In the campaign table, every file contributes 4 curves once the index is excluded, so that column is constant. What does a constant column tell you?', '["That the delivery is free of problems in every other column as well, since six files agreeing exactly is a good sign for the batch","That the files were logged on the same day","That the campaign has aggregated the column incorrectly, because real columns vary","Something about the sender and the export template rather than about the wells"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A column where every entry agrees describes the format and the export rather than any individual file, and the columns worth reading for exceptions are the ones that vary. Reading a constant column as a clean bill of health for the delivery is the classic error in the first option.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-campaign', 7, 'quirks_20.las has a header with irregular spacing and a well name containing a colon, yet it is unexceptional in every column of the campaign table. What is the finding?', '["The campaign has failed to test the header, since header defects never reach the table","A file that is awkward to parse can still be clean data, and a file becomes a problem when the reading comes out wrong rather than when the reading is hard","The parser silently repaired the header and hid a defect","Header quirks are always harmless"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every quirk in that file was handled at read time by a parser that knows the header grammar, so the data behind it is ordinary and the table says so. Difficulty at read time and quality in the data are separate things, which is what the fourth option overstates into a rule.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-campaign', 8, 'basic_20.las is the control file of the teaching set and it carries 17 flagged nulls. What does that show?', '["That a file carrying any flagged nulls at all should be held back and queried with the sender before it is imported","That the control file was built with a defect in it","That scattered nulls are ordinary in log data, so a clean file is not the same thing as an empty one","That 17 is the campaign''s null threshold"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Scattered nulls appear in good log data and their presence is not by itself a defect, which is why the control file has them. Treating any null count above zero as a reason to go back to the sender is the classic error in the first option.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-campaign', 9, 'nullheavy_20.las has 201 depth samples and a flagged null count of 272. What does the fact that 272 exceeds 201 tell you?', '["That the file holds more depth samples than the number its own header declares","That the count runs over the value curves rather than over depths","That the parser has double counted every sample carrying the null flag","That the file is corrupt"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A null count is a count of flagged samples across a file''s value curves, and a file of 201 depths carrying four value curves has far more than 201 sample slots to flag. Reading the excess as corruption is the classic error, and it comes from assuming the unit of the count is depths.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-campaign', 10, 'Which pair of questions should be asked of any campaign aggregate?', '["Which file produced it, and when was that file delivered","How was it computed, and can the computation be repeated","What is it counting, and what does it hide","Is it larger than last time"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Establishing the unit of the count settles what the number is about, and asking what went into the bin settles what the summary threw away on purpose. Both questions have answers on the campaign panel, which shows the composition beside every aggregate.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-campaign', 11, 'The campaign reports 5 for files with a uniform depth step and 1 for dead curves. What are the units of those two counts?', '["Files and curves","Files and files, since both readings are properties of files in the delivery","Curves and curves, since a step is a property of the depth curve and a dead curve is a curve","Samples and curves, because a step verdict is computed from the depth samples"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The uniformity reading counts files and the dead curve reading counts curves, even though that dead curve sits in exactly one file. A reader who knows that one curve is dead and one file converts can slide into believing that one file has a dead curve and one curve was converted, which is the confusion the second and third options embody.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-campaign', 12, 'Why is the number 5 on its own not a reading, while 5 of 6 is?', '["Because 5 is not a round number and cannot be compared across deliveries","Because an aggregate is only meaningful with its set attached","Because the campaign grades this reading as a fraction rather than as a count","Because 6 is the tolerance"]'::jsonb, 1, 'If a seventh file arrived and it stepped uniformly, the count of files without a uniform step would stay at 1 while the count with one rose, and nothing in the bare number would announce that the set had changed. Membership is part of the reading, which is why the composition is shown beside every aggregate.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-campaign', 13, 'What does the reading of 5 files with a uniform depth step hide?', '["The null flag of each file","The number of curves in each of those five files","The sender of the delivery","Which five files they are, and how far the sixth file''s depth column missed by, since the verdict is a yes or a no"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The count says how many passed and names none of them, and because the uniformity check reports a verdict rather than a magnitude, a column that wobbles slightly scores the same as one that wanders badly. The panel answers the first half by showing the composition beside the aggregate.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-campaign', 14, 'In the tier below, a converted depth was graded to 0.01 m. In this tier every graded reading carries a tolerance of zero. Why?', '["Because a converted depth is a continuous quantity produced by float arithmetic, while every reading here is a count, and a count is either right or wrong","Because the pipeline is more accurate when it runs over six files at once","Because the campaign values are small enough to check by eye","Because the Expert tier is graded more strictly than the tier below it as a matter of policy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A tolerance exists to absorb representation and arithmetic differences in a continuous quantity, and a count has none of those. There is no import that leaves you 0.6 of a file needing conversion, so being one off means having counted a different kind of thing rather than having been nearly right.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-campaign', 15, 'Four of the six graded readings are aggregates over the delivery. Which two are readings inside one named file?', '["The dead curve count and the conversion count, since each of those readings points at one named file","The curve count and the uniform step count","The depth samples in wrapped_12 and the flagged nulls in nullheavy_20","The conversion count and the uniform step count"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The sample count of 161 belongs to wrapped_12 and the null count of 272 belongs to nullheavy_20, so both need their file named whenever they are quoted. The other four readings are counts over the six-file set.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-counting-curves', 1, 'The campaign curve count is written as 6 x 4 = 24. What makes the product form available?', '["That the campaign always multiplies the number of files by the number of curves whenever it aggregates a column","That 24 divides by 6","That every one of the six files contributes the same 4 curves once the depth index is excluded","That the files were delivered together"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A sum of six equal terms can be written as a product, and the terms are equal here because each file carries the same curve suite. If one file had shipped a curve short the total would be 23 and the product form would be unavailable, which is why the first option states a rule that does not exist.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-counting-curves', 2, 'Counting the depth index in every file takes the campaign curve count from 24 to 30. Why is the difference exactly 6?', '["Because each file carries exactly one index, and there are six files","Because six of the twenty four curves in the campaign total are depth curves","Because the six files declare six curves each","Because the index curve ends up counted twice in one of the six files"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each file carries one index and the delivery has six files, so the inflation is one per file and equals the file count. The second option inverts the relationship by placing the index curves inside the graded 24, when the graded 24 is precisely the count with them left out.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-counting-curves', 3, 'A learner reports 30 curves for the campaign. What did they count?', '["The curves in five files and the header lines in the sixth","The lines in the six curve sections of the headers added together, which counts the index in each file","The curves plus the dead curve counted twice","The samples divided by the curve count"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Thirty really is the number of curve section lines across the six files, so the answer is produced by careful work from a definition that includes the index. It answers a different question from the graded reading, which counts imported measurements.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-counting-curves', 4, 'What is the argument that the depth column is not a measurement?', '["It holds no numbers of its own","It is never checked by the import pipeline, which tests only the value curves and their units","It is not declared in the curve section","It states where each reading was taken rather than claiming anything about the rock"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Remove the gamma ray from a file and three curves remain, while removing the depth column leaves four unlabelled sequences with no way to place any of them in the hole. That asymmetry is what the word index names, and none of it depends on the column being unchecked or undeclared.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-counting-curves', 5, 'At the Professional tier, feet_20.las was graded at 4 recognised curve kinds out of 5 declared curves. How does that relate to the campaign count of 24?', '["It is unrelated, because kinds and curves are different quantities","It is the identical exclusion applied to one file, since both readings count measurements and leave the index out","It is the reason the campaign count is 30","It applies only to files in foreign units"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both readings ask how many measurements the platform handled, and the index is not a measurement in either, so it is excluded in both. The campaign uses the same rule for the same reason, applied across six files instead of one.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-counting-curves', 6, 'A hypothetical delivery holds twelve files. Nine declare six curves each and three declare four curves each, every file in the ordinary layout with depth first. What curve count would this platform report?', '["54","66, which counts the index curve in every one of the twelve files","72, six curves in each of the twelve files","12, one curve for each file"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nine files at 5 value curves gives 45 and three files at 3 value curves gives 9, so the index-excluded total is 54. The 66 is the same delivery with the index counted in every file, which is the reachable wrong answer this reading is designed to catch.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-counting-curves', 7, 'That same twelve-file delivery is re-exported as twenty four files by splitting each one in half at a depth. The reported count stays 54 while an index-inclusive count rises from 66 to 78. What does that show?', '["That splitting a delivery adds measurements to it","That the platform undercounts split deliveries","That the index-inclusive count is the more sensitive of the two readings and therefore the more useful one to report to a project","That a count including the index moves when nothing has been measured, which makes it the wrong number to report"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The same measured curves arrived in more pieces, so a count that tracks measurements holds still and a count that includes the index tracks how the delivery was cut up. The option calling the index-inclusive count more sensitive mistakes sensitivity to an irrelevant change for usefulness.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-counting-curves', 8, 'Why is a dead curve still counted among the 24 curves imported?', '["Because the campaign cannot tell which curve is dead","Because the count has a tolerance that absorbs it","Because it was delivered and it appears in the registry after import, so leaving it out would make the campaign disagree with what arrived","Because dead curves are usually revived later"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The campaign describes the delivery, and a curve that was declared, read and imported is part of what the delivery contained. A count that quietly dropped it would look tidier while letting a dead column pass unremarked, which is the outcome the separate dead curve reading of 1 exists to prevent.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-counting-curves', 9, 'The campaign imported 24 curves. Which of these does that number claim?', '["That 24 columns were declared, read and produced as curves","That the units of all 24 of those curves were recognised correctly","That the delivery holds the curves the project actually asked for","That 24 curves carry usable measurements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A count answers how many, and says nothing about whether the curves hold data, whether their units are right, or whether they are the curves anybody wanted. The other three options each read a quality claim into a quantity, which is the error the dead curve in this delivery exposes.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-counting-curves', 10, 'In the curve count, how do nullheavy_20.las and basic_20.las compare?', '["nullheavy_20.las contributes fewer curves because one of its curves holds no data","basic_20.las contributes more curves because it has the most depth samples at 301","They are indistinguishable, since both contribute 4 curves","nullheavy_20.las contributes more curves because its null count is the largest in the delivery"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both files contribute 4 curves and the curve count cannot tell them apart, even though one of them holds a curve that promises data and delivers none. The dead curve reading of 1 is what separates them, which is why the two numbers are a finding together and neither is alone.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-counting-curves', 11, 'Why do all six teaching files carry the same curve suite?', '["Because the LAS standard requires every file in one delivery to declare the same curves in the same order and units","Because a delivery from one sender always carries one suite","Because the four value curves are the only curves the parser understands","Because each file was built to isolate one failure mode, so everything except the mode under test is held still"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Holding the suite constant means a reader looking at the null count of one file cannot confuse a completeness problem with a different curve list. The uniformity is an artefact of the teaching design, and reading it as a rule about LAS files is reading the apparatus as the result.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-counting-curves', 12, 'Which of these would make two real files from one sender carry very different curve counts, with nothing wrong in either?', '["A difference in null flag declaration between the two files","One file being a composite carrying the union of every mnemonic in the runs spliced into it","One file being wrapped in the LAS 1.2 style, so that each depth step in it spans several lines","One file arriving with depth in feet while the other arrives in metres, so their depth columns differ"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Composites, different tool strings, processed products and trimmed exports all move the curve count without anything being defective. Wrapping and depth units change the layout and the unit of a file rather than how many curves it declares.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-counting-curves', 13, 'In a delivery whose curve counts are 4, 4, 9, 6, 12 and 5, what does the file contributing 12 prompt you to ask?', '["Whether it is a composite, and whether its extra columns hold measurements","Whether the other five files in the batch came from a different sender entirely","Whether that file ought to be split into smaller pieces before any of it is imported","Whether the campaign total has been computed correctly"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A varying column tells you where to look, and the largest contributor is the one most likely to be a composite carrying the union of several runs. The campaign table then points you at the dead curve and null entries for that file, which is where sentinel filled columns show up.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-counting-curves', 14, 'The campaign total comes back as 20 against an expectation of six files, each known to carry four curves. What is the immediate hypothesis?', '["That the expectation of four curves per file was wrong","That one of the six files shipped a curve short of the expected suite","That the index was excluded twice in the aggregation step","That a file failed to load, since 20 is 5 x 4"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In a delivery where every file contributes equally, the total is proportional to the file count, so a shortfall of exactly one file''s worth reads as a missing file. Where files contribute unequally the same total supports several stories and the per-file rows are the only way to choose between them.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-counting-curves', 15, 'You are reporting the campaign curve count to a colleague. What should travel with the number?', '["The date the delivery arrived","That the index is excluded, and the readings that qualify it, such as the dead curve and the two file level exceptions","The sender''s name and the field name","The largest and smallest depth sample counts in the delivery"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A bare 24 invites the reader to conclude that twenty four usable curves arrived, which the count does not support. Saying depth excluded also stops a reader reconstructing 30 from the curve sections and believing the campaign disagrees with the files.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-finding-the-exceptions', 1, 'The campaign field for files needing a depth unit conversion returns which value for the six teaching files?', '["5, matching the count of files with a uniform depth step","6, because every file in the delivery was checked","1, and the file is feet_20.las","0, because the pipeline converts curves and not files"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Five files declare a metric depth unit and one does not, so the field counts one file, feet_20.las. Reporting 5 confuses this field with the uniform step field, which is a pass count over a different test.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-finding-the-exceptions', 2, 'When you look for a file that needs a depth unit conversion without opening the files, which signal is the finding rather than corroboration or a verdict?', '["The depth unit declared in each file''s header, read as one column across the batch","The pipeline''s converted flag, which reports the conclusion the importer already reached","The depth range, since a foot file carries numerically larger start and stop values","The sample count, which changes when a depth column is restated in metres"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The declared unit is the evidence, the depth range only corroborates it, and the converted flag is a verdict that can be false for the wrong reason when a unit string is unrecognised. Treating the flag as evidence is the classic error, because it makes the campaign confirm itself.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-finding-the-exceptions', 3, 'More than one curve inside feet_20.las was restated by the pipeline. What does the campaign field record for that file?', '["The number of curves restated, since the conversion is decided one curve at a time","One for each unit string that changed, added across the file","Nothing, because curve level conversion belongs to the Professional tier","One, because the field counts files rather than curves"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The field asks how many files needed a conversion, so feet_20.las contributes 1 however many of its curves were restated. Answering with a curve count is a correct answer to a question nobody asked, and a tolerance of zero gives it no credit.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-finding-the-exceptions', 4, 'The campaign field for files with a uniform depth step returns which value?', '["1, because irregular_20.las is the only file whose depth column is not uniform","5, the number of files that pass the test","6, because every file in the set has a depth column","0, because the test returns a step"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The label names the files that have a uniform step, and 5 of the 6 do. Reporting 1 is the named trap: it is the failure count, which is a correct answer to a different question.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-finding-the-exceptions', 5, 'A learner reports 1 for the uniform step field. What is the most accurate description of what happened?', '["They miscounted, because only one of the six files was actually tested for uniformity","They answered the failure count correctly, which is a different question from the one the field asks","They read the value of the dead curve field by mistake","They confused files with curves"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both 5 and 1 describe the same campaign, and only one of them answers the field as worded. The fields on either side of this one count exceptions, which is what makes the polarity easy to carry across by accident.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-finding-the-exceptions', 6, 'irregular_20.las fails the uniformity test. What does the importer do with it?', '["It repairs the depth column by resampling every curve onto the average step, so that the file ends up matching the other five","It rejects the file, since no downstream tool can use it","It reports the step declared in the file header instead","It imports the file with its 121 depth samples and returns no step, which is an explicit refusal to report a spacing"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The file is usable and is imported as logged, and the one thing that changes is that the step field comes back empty. Nothing is repaired, because the campaign records what arrived rather than editing it.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-finding-the-exceptions', 7, 'How is a dead curve defined for the campaign''s dead curve field?', '["A curve with no finite samples at all","A curve whose flagged samples exceed a stated proportion of its column","A curve declared in the header but absent from the data grid","A curve whose flagged samples fall in one continuous interval"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The test is exact and carries no threshold, so a single finite sample keeps a curve out of the count. Treating a dead curve as merely a very null one is the classic error, and a fuzzy definition cannot be added up across a batch.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-finding-the-exceptions', 8, 'The campaign reports 1 dead curve. That count sits over which population?', '["The six files in the delivery, exactly one of which contains a dead curve","The 4 value curves of nullheavy_20.las alone","The 24 curves the campaign imported across the six files","The 201 samples of the affected column"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The field counts curves, and its denominator is the campaign''s 24 imported curves, so the finding reads as 1 dead curve in 24. The file level reading is where the total came from rather than what it is a count of.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-finding-the-exceptions', 9, 'Why does the dead curve field count curves when the exception fields beside it count files?', '["Because a file cannot be counted once a curve inside it has failed","Because the campaign cannot attribute a dead curve to a file","Because curves are the only population with a fixed denominator","Because a file level flag would report the same value for a file with one dead curve as for a file with several, which loses the size of the problem"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Deadness is a property of a curve, so counting curves preserves the difference between a scattered problem and a collapsed one. The campaign still reports which file the count came from.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-finding-the-exceptions', 10, 'Three graded fields each locate a file that differs from the rest. Which files do they locate?', '["nullheavy_20.las alone, because it is the file that carries every defect the campaign was built to find","feet_20.las, irregular_20.las and nullheavy_20.las, one for each of the three fields","feet_20.las and irregular_20.las, because the dead curve field counts curves","basic_20.las, quirks_20.las and wrapped_12.las"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each field returns an exception of size one, and each one falls in a different file. No single file in this delivery is the bad one, which is why the campaign is worth running over all six.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-finding-the-exceptions', 11, 'Why does the field that returns 24 name no file the way the three exception fields do?', '["Because every file contributes exactly 4 curves once the depth index is excluded, so nothing departs from the pattern","Because curve counts are recorded per campaign and never per file","Because excluding the depth index removes the differences between files","Because 24 is a total and totals never locate anything"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Six files at 4 curves each is 24 exactly, so the field confirms the shape of the delivery rather than isolating a member of it. A field with no exception in it is still a result, because it is what lets a reader stop worrying about the count.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-finding-the-exceptions', 12, 'In the sense this module uses the word, what is the product of a campaign run?', '["The totals, which confirm that the delivery is the size you expected it to be","The full per file table, since that is what the panel renders","The list of exceptions, each with a file name and a consequence","The receipt that six files each delivered 4 curves"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The totals are a receipt that stops being informative once it agrees with expectation, while each exception is short, specific and actionable. Quoting the totals as the finding is the common failure, because they are the numbers a report leads with.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-finding-the-exceptions', 13, 'Two situations both produce an exception count of zero. Which pair is it?', '["A genuinely uniform delivery, and a check that is not doing anything","A delivery that contains a single exception, and a delivery that contains a great many","A tolerance that is set too tight, and a tolerance that is set far too loose","A batch read out of order, and a batch read twice"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The output looks identical in both cases, so a zero is only a finding when you can see the denominator it was measured over. Keeping a file you know fails the test in the run turns an unfalsifiable zero into a checked one.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-finding-the-exceptions', 14, 'A field reports that every file in a delivery needs a depth unit conversion. What has it most likely found?', '["A catastrophic delivery that should be rejected in full","The same finding as one exception, scaled up by the batch size","A denominator error, because a count cannot equal its own population","The convention of the delivery, or a miscalibrated test, rather than a defect in any one file"]'::jsonb, 3, 'When every file behaves the same way nothing is the odd one out, so the response is one decision applied to the whole source and a note recorded for next time. A check that fails everything is more often miscalibrated than a delivery is uniformly foreign.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-finding-the-exceptions', 15, 'Why is a record of campaign counts across successive deliveries worth keeping?', '["Because the graded fields change their value whenever the size of the batch changes","Because movement in a field is the signal, and one run gives only a snapshot","Because a baseline removes the need to run the checks each time","Because deliveries from a single source will always return the same counts on every field"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A field that has always returned zero and now returns something has caught a real change on the day it happened. Without a baseline there is nothing to compare a single run against.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-aggregates-hide-composition', 1, 'What does the graded value of 272 count?', '["272 bad readings scattered evenly through the file''s curves, which is what a large null total usually means","Flagged samples across the 4 value curves of nullheavy_20.las, each of 201 samples","Flagged samples in every column of the file, including the depth index","Depth samples in nullheavy_20.las that carry no measurement in any curve"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The count covers the value curves only, with the depth index excluded, and it uses the null flag the file itself declares. Reading 272 as a scatter of bad readings is the classic error, because the total is consistent with several very different arrangements.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-aggregates-hide-composition', 2, 'A reader that assumes the common null value instead of the one nullheavy_20.las declares reports what?', '["A total slightly below 272, because some flagged cells still match","272, because the parser corrects a mismatched sentinel automatically","A total above 272, because real readings are mistaken for flags","Zero nulls, and it hands downstream statistics a column of large negative values"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The file declares -9999, so a reader looking for a different sentinel finds no matches at all and reports 0. The failure is silent, since the file parses and the columns line up while every statistic drawn from it is poisoned.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-aggregates-hide-composition', 3, 'How does nullheavy_20.las compare with the rest of the campaign on flagged nulls?', '["It is more than an order of magnitude above the next largest, which is 17 in basic_20.las","It is the largest because nullheavy_20.las holds the most depth samples of the six files","It is roughly twice the next largest count in the delivery","It is the largest, and basic_20.las at 301 samples is close behind"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Five files sit in the low tens and one sits at 272, on a file that is not the largest in the set, since basic_20.las carries 301 depth samples and reports 17 nulls. The gap is what isolates the file the same way the other exception fields isolate theirs.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-aggregates-hide-composition', 4, 'Which set of per curve null counts belongs to nullheavy_20.las?', '["GR 201 of 201, RHOB 0 of 201, NPHI 71 of 201, DT 0 of 201, so the dead curve is GR","GR 71 of 201, RHOB 201 of 201, and no flagged samples in NPHI or DT","GR 71 of 201, RHOB 0 of 201, NPHI 201 of 201, DT 0 of 201","GR 0 of 201, with all 272 in NPHI in V/V"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The four rows are GR 71 of 201, RHOB 0 of 201, NPHI 201 of 201 and DT 0 of 201, and they add to 272 exactly. Two of the four curves carry no flagged samples at all, which no reading of the total would suggest.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-aggregates-hide-composition', 5, 'Which curve contributes 201 of the 272, and in what unit?', '["NPHI in V/V, which has 201 nulls of 201 and no finite samples at all","GR in GAPI, which has 71 nulls of 201 and keeps the rest","RHOB in G/C3, which has 0 nulls of 201","DT in US/M, whose flagged samples the campaign does not report"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every one of NPHI''s 201 samples holds the declared flag, so it is the campaign''s one dead curve and it supplies the larger part of the file''s total. GR contributes the other 71 of the 272.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-aggregates-hide-composition', 6, 'How many of nullheavy_20.las''s four value curves are fully populated?', '["One, GR in GAPI, which keeps most of its readings","None, because the file carries the largest null total in the delivery at 272","Two, RHOB in G/C3 and DT in US/M, each with 0 nulls of 201","Three, because only the dead curve is affected"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Half of this file''s value curves have no flagged samples anywhere, which is the fact the aggregate hides most completely. Assuming a large total means every curve is damaged is the usual reading and it is wrong here.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-aggregates-hide-composition', 7, 'If every one of GR''s 71 nulls of 201 were filled in and nothing else changed, what happens to the file''s null total?', '["It falls from 272 to 71, because GR is the curve that carries most of the total","It stays at 272, because the dead curve is counted separately","It falls to 0, because the other curves have 0 nulls of 201","It falls from 272 to 201, and the largest problem in the file is untouched"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Removing GR''s contribution leaves NPHI''s 201 of 201 behind, which is the item that actually needs a query to the supplier. Expecting the total to fall to 71 is the classic error of assuming the scattered nulls dominate.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-aggregates-hide-composition', 8, 'A colleague receives the number 272 and nothing else. Which two things do they not know?', '["Which file it belongs to, and how many curves that file holds","What kind of missing this is, and how concentrated it is","Whether the count is exact, and whether the flag came from the header","How the parser stores flagged values, and which unit each curve carries"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both questions change what a reader does next, and the total answers neither. Nothing in the number is false, which is why it misleads without ever being wrong.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-aggregates-hide-composition', 9, 'What is the relationship between the dead curve count of 1 and the null total of 272?', '["They are separate findings about the delivery, so a severity measure can add them together to size the problem","The dead curve is counted after the flagged cells are removed, so no cell appears twice","The dead curve accounts for 201 of the 272, so the two fields are not independent readings","They describe different files, since the dead curve field covers all 24 curves"]'::jsonb, 2, 'One absence is counted twice, once as a curve and once as 201 cells, and the two fields move together when it is resolved. Treating them as independent findings is the named trap, and it is what makes people add them.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-aggregates-hide-composition', 10, 'Why can the dead curve field and the null total not be added into a severity score?', '["Because the same 201 cells would be counted twice, inflating the file''s apparent badness","Because the null total already excludes any curve that has no finite samples","Because the two fields use different denominators and cannot be compared","Because severity must be computed one curve at a time"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The dead curve is 201 of the 272, so any sum of the two fields double counts that absence. A pair of numbers where fixing one changes the other has to be reported together with the relationship stated.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-aggregates-hide-composition', 11, 'Beyond the size of the number, what separates NPHI''s 201 nulls of 201 from GR''s 71 nulls of 201?', '["NPHI''s flagged samples are contiguous while GR''s are scattered, and that difference in shape is the only thing the campaign records","NPHI was flagged with a different null value from the rest of the file","NPHI is a very null curve and GR is a slightly null one, so the two of them differ only in degree and belong together in the same note","NPHI has no finite samples, so the question is whether the curve exists anywhere, while GR is usable with its gaps characterised"]'::jsonb, 3, 'One finding is about whether a curve was ever delivered and the other is about data quality inside a working curve, so they go to different people. Reading the pair as two sizes of the same problem is the classic error this module exists to break.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-aggregates-hide-composition', 12, 'What do you ask the supplier about the absent neutron curve?', '["Whether the null value declared in the header was really intended to apply to that column as well as to the other three","Whether a neutron tool was run, which file carries the curve if it was, and for a written statement if it was not","Whether the file can be redelivered with the empty column stripped out of both the header and the data grid, so that nothing downstream meets the mnemonic","Whether the curve can be reconstructed from RHOB in G/C3"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Either a corrected file or a recorded absence closes the item, and an unanswered query leaves a mnemonic that downstream software still believes in. Removing the column from the delivered file would destroy the record of what the supplier sent.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-aggregates-hide-composition', 13, 'Which of the two findings in nullheavy_20.las goes out first, and on what basis?', '["The GR gaps, because 71 nulls of 201 can be characterised from the file you already hold without asking anybody","The neutron query, because 201 of 201 is the larger of the two numbers and therefore the larger problem","Whichever of the two the interpreter happens to ask you about first, since both are open on the file","The neutron query, because it has the longest turnaround and depends on someone else going to look"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Priority follows turnaround rather than the size of the number, since a query that depends on someone finding a run ticket may not close at all if it starts late. Sorting by the count alone would only tell you the file looks bad.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-aggregates-hide-composition', 14, 'Which four elements make a reported total defensible?', '["The number with what it counts, the population, the denominator, and the largest single contributor","The number, the file it came from, the date on which it was computed, and the name of the person who computed it","The number, its unit, the tolerance it is graded on, and the name of the field it was recorded under","The number and its denominator, which between them are enough for any reader to act on without more detail"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The fourth element is the one people leave out, and it is the one that carries the meaning. When a breakdown has four rows you give all four, and when it has many you give the largest contributor and the shape of the rest.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-aggregates-hide-composition', 15, 'You have a total but have not examined its breakdown. What is the honest way to report it?', '["Report the total as a finished finding, since the number itself is correct","Report the total with an estimated breakdown so the reader has the shape of it","Report the total and note that the composition has not been examined","Withhold the total until the breakdown is available"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A total presented as a total, with its limits stated, is honest, and the same total presented as a finished finding is not. Quoting an aggregate with no composition and no warning is the habit this module is built to break.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-wrapped-file', 1, 'In LAS 1.2, what does a version section declaring WRAP YES mean for the data section?', '["Each curve is written as a complete block, one curve after another, from the first depth to the last depth in the file.","The depth value is repeated at the start of every continuation line so that a reader can regroup the records.","One depth step''s readings continue across several physical lines, with the values still in curve order.","The curve values for one depth step are written in reverse curve order."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Wrapping is a statement about where the writer put line breaks. The order of the values, the curve contract they follow and the treatment of nulls are all unchanged.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-wrapped-file', 2, 'Why did LAS 1.2 offer wrapped mode at all?', '["Wrapping compressed the file so that large logging runs could be transferred over slow links.","The standard required one curve per line so that columns could be identified by position.","Wrapping marked which depth steps had been quality controlled.","A text line was expected to stay inside 80 characters, and a run with many curves cannot fit that many formatted numbers into that width."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The 80 character limit was the width of a terminal, a punched card and most printers of the period. LAS 2.0 dropped the need for it, and wrapped files survive in archives.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-wrapped-file', 3, 'Given one data line taken out of the middle of wrapped_12.las, how can you tell whether it starts a depth record or continues one?', '["You cannot, because wrapping is declared once in the version section and no marker appears in the data lines.","Each continuation line begins with a continuation character, so the line alone tells you whether it starts a record or carries one on from the line above it.","Wrapping is declared in the curve section, which is where the column layout is defined.","A blank line separates one depth step from the next."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The WRAP line in the version section governs the whole data block and there is no per line marker. Expecting a continuation character is the classic error, and it is why the version section is read first.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-wrapped-file', 4, 'How many curves does wrapped_12.las declare, and how many does it contribute to the campaign curve total?', '["5 curves including the depth index, and 5 curves toward the campaign total, because the reshape divides by 5 and the campaign should agree with the reshape.","4 curves including the depth index, so 3 curves once the depth index is excluded from the count.","5 curves including the depth index, and 4 curves once the depth index is excluded.","161 curves, one for each depth sample."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The reshape divides by 5 because the depth index occupies a token slot, while the campaign counts 4 because it is measuring how much log data arrived. The two counts answer different questions on the same file.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-wrapped-file', 5, 'What does the parser do with the WRAP value it reads from the version section?', '["It records the value and reports it, and the data reader never consults it.","It reads WRAP and switches the data reader into an unwrapping branch that groups the physical lines belonging to each depth step before the reshape.","It ignores the version section entirely, so the WRAP value never reaches a reader at all.","It uses WRAP to decide how many lines to skip after each depth value."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Assuming an unwrapping branch exists is the classic error, since there is no such branch in the engine. The file''s claim about its layout is surfaced to a person and is not an input to the reassembly.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-wrapped-file', 6, 'What does the data reader do with each line after the ~A marker?', '["It records how many tokens each line held so that the reshape can group the lines belonging to one depth step.","It reads the first token as a depth and the rest of that line as the depth''s curve values.","It pads short lines with the declared null so every line holds a full record.","It drops blanks and comment lines, splits the rest on whitespace, converts every piece to a number, and pushes the results onto one flat stream."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Line boundaries are consumed and discarded in the split, which is what makes wrapped and unwrapped files the same code path.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-wrapped-file', 7, 'When does the parser substitute the file''s declared null value?', '["On the full precision value before the cast to 32-bit float, so a token written as -999.2500 matches a declaration of -999.25.","After the cast to 32-bit float, so trailing zeros in the file prevent the match.","It drops null tokens from the stream, which shortens it.","It leaves nulls as numbers and excludes them later by a threshold."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dropping null tokens would change the token count and break the divisibility the reshape depends on. The substitution happens in place, so a null occupies a slot exactly like any other value.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-wrapped-file', 8, 'The data section of wrapped_12.las holds 805 numeric tokens and the curve section declares 5 curves. What does the reshape produce?', '["201.25 samples per curve, because the depth index is excluded from the count of curves before the division is done.","161 samples per curve, with no remainder.","483 samples per curve, one for each line in the data block.","805 samples per curve, since every token is a sample."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Dividing by 4 rather than 5 gives 201.25 and is the classic error, because the depth index occupies a token slot in every record. The clean division by 5 is also the arithmetic guard passing.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-wrapped-file', 9, 'Why does the reshape divide the token stream by 5 for wrapped_12.las when the campaign credits the file with 4 curves?', '["The campaign counts 5 curves for this file, so the reshape and the campaign already agree with one another.","The wrap depth of 3 lines adds a curve to the divisor.","LAS 1.2 always declares one more curve than it holds.","The depth index takes a token slot in every record."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The depth index occupies a token slot in every record, so the reshape counts it even though the campaign curve total excludes it. A count is defined by the question it answers.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-wrapped-file', 10, 'The data block of wrapped_12.las holds 483 lines. What is 483 divided by 161?', '["3 curves, since dividing the line count by the sample count returns the number of curves.","3 lines per depth step, which is the wrap layout of the file recovered by division.","3 tokens per line, which the parser uses to group records.","The wrap depth declared in the version section."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The parser never computes this division and never needs it. You compute it as a reader, and it answers a question about layout rather than about content.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-wrapped-file', 11, 'A reader built on one record per line is pointed at wrapped_12.las. What does it report, and why is the error that size?', '["A parse error, because the line count is not a multiple of the declared curve count and the reader checks that before reporting anything.","805 samples, since it counts every numeric token in the data section.","483 samples, which is three times the truth because the wrap layout is three lines deep.","161 samples, because line counting and token counting agree."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The size of the overcount is the wrap depth, so it is invisible from the value alone unless you know what the depth frame should hold. A line counting reader has no divisibility guard to save it.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-wrapped-file', 12, 'wrapped_12.las declares a start of 1500 m, a stop of 1580 m and a step of 0.5 m. What sample count does that frame imply?', '["160 samples, from (1580 - 1500) / 0.5, which is the count the parser reports.","161 samples, from (1580 - 1500) / 0.5 + 1.","483 samples, matching the line count of the data block.","The frame cannot be used, since a header is a claim rather than a measurement."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Forgetting the plus one is the classic fencepost error and gives 160. The frame is a claim, and the point of the check is that a claim and a measurement can be compared.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-wrapped-file', 13, 'Why is the header frame check worth running when the reshape has already produced 161 samples?', '["It is faster than counting tokens, which is its only advantage over the reshape.","It replaces the reshape, so the parser could use the header instead.","It reaches the same 161 by a route that never touches the token stream.","It confirms the wrap depth of three lines per depth step."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Agreement between two independent derivations is evidence rather than repetition, which is what lets you report the number. A disagreement means either the header lies or the reader misread the data.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-wrapped-file', 14, 'Under what two conditions does flatten and reshape give the right answer?', '["The token count is an exact multiple of the declared curve count, and every record is complete.","The file declares WRAP NO, and the curve count is 5 or fewer.","The line count is an exact multiple of the curve count, and the file is LAS 2.0.","The depth column is uniform, and the null is declared as -999.25."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Neither condition mentions wrapping, which is the whole point of the design. Both are statements about the token stream rather than about the layout of the lines.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-wrapped-file', 15, 'One copy of a file is cut off part way through its last depth step. A second copy is cut cleanly at a record boundary, so whole depth steps are missing from the end. What happens to each?', '["Both stop the parse, because the guard compares the reshaped sample count against the header depth frame before it accepts the file for import.","The first stops the parse, and the second parses successfully as a shorter log.","Both parse successfully, since the reshape uses only the tokens that are present.","The first parses as a shorter log, and the second stops the parse."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The part way truncation leaves a token count that is not a multiple of the curve count, so the divisibility guard fires. A clean truncation preserves divisibility and is invisible to that guard, which is why the header frame check is a separate reader habit rather than part of the parse.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-campaign-workflow', 1, 'What was the unit of work at each of the three Well Data Manager tiers?', '["Every tier works one file, and the tiers differ only in how many checks they run on it.","The Associate tier ran a campaign, and the two tiers above it narrowed to one file.","The Associate tier read and quality controlled one file, the Professional tier imported one file into the project''s units and vocabulary, and this tier runs the delivery as a campaign.","All three tiers work a delivery, at increasing levels of detail."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The campaign runs the Professional pipeline unchanged over a set. Nothing new is tested at this tier, and everything new is in the reading.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-campaign-workflow', 2, 'The campaign reports 24 curves imported. Which reconciliation confirms that reading?', '["Six files at 5 curves each gives 30, which is the total once the depth index is counted as a curve in every file of the delivery.","Six files at 4 curves each, with the depth index excluded from every file''s count.","The campaign totals the samples column rather than the curves column.","Twenty four is the curve count of nullheavy_20 alone."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Counting the depth index in every file gives 30 and is the classic overcount this course flags at all three tiers. Adding the per file column and comparing it with the total is the cheapest check on the page.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-campaign-workflow', 3, 'Why is a campaign count reported without a file name treated as an incomplete finding?', '["A count without a name cannot be graded by the capstone.","The panel refuses to display a count until its file is named.","Nobody schedules work against the sentence one file needs conversion, while feet_20.las needs conversion names a file somebody can open.","A count without a name is arithmetically unreliable."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Four of the six readings are counts where exactly one file differs, and the name is what turns the number into work a project can schedule.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-campaign-workflow', 4, 'Which file is the exception behind each of the three exception readings in this delivery?', '["feet_20.las converts, nullheavy_20.las has no uniform step, and the dead curve is in irregular_20.las instead.","feet_20.las converts, irregular_20.las has no uniform step, and the dead curve is in nullheavy_20.las.","irregular_20.las converts, feet_20.las has no uniform depth step, and the dead curve is in quirks_20.las.","basic_20.las converts, wrapped_12.las has no uniform step, and the dead curve is in feet_20.las."]'::jsonb, 1, 'feet_20.las is the only foreign unit file, irregular_20.las is why 5 of 6 rather than all 6 are uniform, and the dead NPHI sits in nullheavy_20.las.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-campaign-workflow', 5, 'How do the 272 flagged nulls in nullheavy_20.las break down by curve?', '["201 in GR out of 201 samples, 0 in RHOB, 71 in NPHI out of 201 samples, and 0 in DT.","272 in NPHI alone, which is why that curve is dead.","The 272 are spread evenly across the four curves.","71 in GR, 0 in RHOB, 201 in NPHI, and 0 in DT."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each curve is measured against 201 samples, so RHOB and DT are complete, GR is missing about a third of its readings and NPHI has no data at all. The single number 272 shows none of that, which is why every aggregate travels with its composition.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-campaign-workflow', 6, 'How are the dead curve count of 1 and the null count of 272 related?', '["The dead curve is 201 of the 272, so only 71 are scattered nulls in a curve that has data.","They are independent readings, so the file carries a dead curve as well as 272 scattered nulls spread through its other curves.","They are the same reading counted twice, so one of them should be dropped.","The 272 excludes the dead curve, which is counted separately."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Reading them as independent overstates the damage by 201 and is the classic error here. They are graded as separate fields and they describe overlapping evidence.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-campaign-workflow', 7, 'What null value does nullheavy_20.las declare, and why does it matter?', '["-999.25, the value the other teaching files use, which the parser applies to every file in the set.","-999.2500, which the parser treats as a different value from -999.25.","It declares no null value, so the parser falls back to the common value of -999.25.","-9999, which the parser honours because the flag is read rather than assumed."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Assuming the common value rather than reading the declaration is the classic error, and on this file it would leave 272 nulls uncounted. Null substitution happens at full precision before the cast to 32-bit float, so -999.2500 and -999.25 do match.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-campaign-workflow', 8, 'A campaign summary reports 30 curves imported across the six files. What has happened?', '["The depth index was counted as a curve in every file, since six files at 5 curves each gives 30.","Six extra curves were found by the pipeline that the per file rows in the campaign table do not show.","The samples column was totalled instead of the curves column.","Two files were counted twice."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The depth index is excluded from the campaign curve total, so the correct reconciliation is six files at 4 curves each. This is the overcount check 1 exists to catch.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-campaign-workflow', 9, 'A campaign row shows 483 samples for wrapped_12.las. What is that number?', '["A correct reading, because a wrapped file holds one depth sample on each physical line of its data block.","The count of numeric tokens the data section holds, which is 805 for this file.","The count of physical lines in the data block reported as a sample count.","A rounding error introduced when the graded value of 161 depth samples was rounded."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Confusing lines with samples is the classic error on this file, and it means every curve was misassembled. The graded value is 161 samples.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-campaign-workflow', 10, 'Which set is the six graded fields of the Expert capstone, in capstone order?', '["24 curves, 1 file converting, 1 dead curve, 1 file without a uniform step, 161 samples, 272 nulls, since every exception field names the file that fails.","30 curves, 1 file converting, 1 dead curve, 5 uniform files, 483 samples, 272 nulls.","24 curves, 2 files converting, 1 dead curve, 5 uniform files, 161 samples, and 71 nulls.","24 curves, 1 converting, 1 dead curve, 5 uniform files, 161 samples, 272 nulls."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Entering 1 for the uniform step field is the classic loss, because that field counts the files that pass. The other five fields each name a count of the thing being reported.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-campaign-workflow', 11, 'What tolerance does each of the six capstone fields carry, and why?', '["The four smaller counts carry a tolerance of zero, while the sample count and the null count allow a small margin because they are larger numbers.","Every field carries a tolerance of zero, because all six are counts and a count is either the number of things or it is not.","Every field allows a tolerance of 0.01, in the way the two converted depth fields did at the Professional tier below this one.","The tolerances vary from zero on the curve total to 1 on the null count."]'::jsonb, 1, 'There is no margin anywhere on this paper. Compare the tier below, where two depth fields tolerated 0.01 m because a metre is a continuous quantity.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-campaign-workflow', 12, 'The capstone field Files with a uniform depth step grades 5. Why is 1 the classic loss?', '["The field counts the files that pass, while every other exception field on the paper counts the file that fails.","Because irregular_20.las is the exception, and a paper that names exceptions everywhere else should name this one too, which is what makes the mistake reasonable.","Because the grader accepts either the passing count or the failing count.","Because 5 of 6 is displayed as a ratio rather than a count."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Reading the direction of the field is the whole difficulty. The tile shows it as 5 of 6, and the exception is irregular_20.las.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-campaign-workflow', 13, 'The Professional capstone graded 2 for feet_20''s unit conversion and the Expert capstone grades 1 for files needing depth unit conversion. Why do they differ?', '["The Expert pipeline converts fewer curves than the Professional pipeline did, because it works at delivery scale.","The Professional field counted curves within one file, the depth column and the sonic, while the Expert field counts files across the delivery.","One of the two conversions was withdrawn when the campaign was run.","The Expert field counts only curves whose unit carries a length."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The same file supports both answers because the two fields count different things. Carrying the per curve answer up into a per file field is the classic error.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-campaign-workflow', 14, 'In what order must a learner clear the gates before the Expert capstone unlocks?', '["Every lesson read, then each module quiz passed at 70 percent, then the final exam at 75 percent, with the capstone available at any point after that.","The capstone unlocks on enrollment, and the module quizzes and the final exam confirm the result afterwards.","Every lesson read, then each module quiz passed at 75 percent, then the final exam at 70 percent.","Any three module quizzes, then the final exam."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The server enforces the sequence, and three consecutive failed quiz attempts trigger a 24 hour cooldown. Passing the capstone grants the Expert certification in Well Data Manager.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-campaign-workflow', 15, 'Which of these is inside what well data management can guarantee?', '["That what is missing is counted rather than averaged away, and that a curve with no data is named as such.","That a gamma ray curve which parses cleanly and carries no nulls came from a well calibrated tool.","That the file describes the well its header names.","That a dead curve can be recovered by interpolation."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The guarantees are about handling rather than about truth. A dead curve is fixed by a redelivery, since nothing in the file can be interpolated into data that was never recorded.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'Compared with the two tiers below it, what has changed at this tier?', '["The checks have been replaced with stricter versions of themselves, since an Expert tier tests the same material more severely","The unit of work, which is now the delivery rather than the file, while the checks themselves are the ones you already know","The pipeline, which now runs a different parser","The tolerances, which are now looser to accommodate six files"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every campaign reading rolls up a check from the Associate or Professional tier, so the tests are familiar and the aggregation is the new skill. The tolerances move in the other direction, since every graded reading here is a count with no margin at all.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'An aggregate is dominated by one member of the set. What is wrong with reporting it alone?', '["It cannot be reproduced by a second reader working from the same delivery","It will change if the files in the set are read in a different order","It carries no unit that a reader could sensibly attach to the number itself","It describes that member while appearing to describe the whole set"]'::jsonb, 3, 'An evenly built aggregate such as the curve count summarises the delivery, while one carried by a single member is really a statement about that member wearing the delivery''s name. Showing the composition beside the number is what prevents the misreading.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'Why does the campaign count files with a uniform depth step rather than averaging the step sizes across the delivery?', '["Because an average would absorb an irregular depth column into a plausible looking number and hide it, while a count cannot cancel","Because the files have different depth units","Because averages are harder to compute over six files","Because the step is not a number"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Counts do not cancel and averages do, so an irregular column that scores as a firm no in a count would disappear into a mean. The choice of a count over an average is deliberate rather than incidental.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'A learner reports 6 curves for a single one of the teaching files. What has gone wrong?', '["They counted one of the dead curves in the delivery twice","They added the null flag as a curve","They counted the index as a measurement","They read the campaign total and divided it by six"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each file declares five curves and contributes four once the index is excluded, so a 6 cannot be reached by miscounting a correct definition. Because the readings here are counts with zero tolerance, being one off signals a wrong definition rather than a near miss.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'Which graded reading of this tier has no exception in it?', '["Files needing a depth unit conversion, which is 1 and names the only file in foreign depth units","Files with a uniform depth step, which is 5 because one file of the six has no uniform step","Dead curves detected, which is 1","Curves imported across the campaign, which is 24, because every file contributes equally"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Three of the readings each point at exactly one file that differs from the rest, and the curve count is the one where every member contributes the same 4. That makes it the control column against which an exception looks like one.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'A seventh file arrives from the same sender carrying the same curve suite. What happens to the graded curve count and its tolerance?', '["The count stays 24 and the tolerance stays zero","The count becomes 28 and the tolerance stays zero","The count becomes 28 and the tolerance widens because the set is larger","The count becomes 35 because the index is counted for the new file"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Four more value curves take the total to 28, and adding members to a set does not make a count approximate, so the tolerance is unchanged. A tolerance absorbs arithmetic and representation differences, and a count has neither.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'One of the six files had shipped without one of its four value curves. What is the campaign curve count, and can it still be written as a product?', '["24, and yes, because the missing curve is imported as a dead column","20, and yes, because five files contribute","23, and no, because the files no longer contribute equally","23, and yes, because 23 is 6 x 4 less one"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Five files at 4 curves plus one at 3 gives 23, and a sum of unequal terms has no product form. The last option keeps the product language for a sum that no longer has one, which is the tidy-looking error to avoid.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'A hypothetical delivery contributes 4, 4, 9, 6, 12 and 5 curves with the index already excluded. What is the campaign total, and what would a reader who counted the index report?', '["40 and 46","40 and 40, because the index is not declared in these files","46 and 52, counting the index in the contributions given","40 and 45, adding one index to the delivery as a whole"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The six contributions sum to 40, and counting one index in each of the six files adds 6 to give 46. The difference is the file count, which describes how the delivery was cut up rather than how much was measured.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'The samples column of the campaign table runs 301, 151, 121, 201, 81 and 161. How should that column be read?', '["As evidence that the smaller files are incomplete and should be queried with the sender","As a column that varies in every row without any of that variation being a problem","As the campaign''s most important exception column","As a column that must be equalised across the files before any aggregate means anything"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Varying sample counts are ordinary, so this column carries no exception and no finding. Reading a smaller file as a defective one is the classic error, and it comes from expecting every column in a campaign table to work like an exception column.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'Excluding the index from the curve count is sometimes read as a claim that the depth column does not matter. What is the answer to that?', '["The index does not matter, which is why it is excluded","The index is excluded only when it is uniform","The index is a measurement, and the exclusion is a convention with no reasoning behind it","The index gets more scrutiny than any value curve, since three of the six graded readings are about it, and the exclusion places it in a different category rather than in a lower one"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The conversion count, the uniform step count and the wrapped sample count are all readings about depth columns, so the index is reported in the columns where it belongs. Excluding it from a count of measurements is a statement about category rather than about importance.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'Which teaching file is the control of the set?', '["quirks_20.las, because its data section is unremarkable","wrapped_12.las, because its unusual layout produces an ordinary row","basic_20.las","feet_20.las, because it is the only file that converts"]'::jsonb, 2, 'basic_20.las is the file with nothing wrong in it, and it is the row every other row is read against. Without a control, a reader cannot tell which entries in the table are properties of these files and which are properties of LAS files in general.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'The campaign panel shows the composition beside every aggregate. Why?', '["Because an aggregate on its own can be believed too quickly, and the composition is what lets a reader see whether the count is spread across the delivery or carried by one member","Because the aggregates are approximate and the composition is exact","Because the panel cannot compute totals without showing their parts","Because the capstone grades the composition"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Pairing the number with its parts turns a headline into a finding and answers the second of the two questions before the reader has to ask it. The aggregates are exact counts, so the second option misstates why the pairing exists.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'Why should the file name travel with the readings 161 and 272?', '["Because the capstone grades the file name","Because both files are unusual","Because those two readings are properties of one file each","Because the campaign cannot compute either reading without the file name"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The sample count of 161 belongs to wrapped_12 and the null count of 272 belongs to nullheavy_20, while the other four graded readings are counts over the six-file set. A bare 161 reads as a fact about the delivery, which it is not.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'Why is a summary that gives one verdict per file less useful than one giving counts across the batch?', '["Because the comparison across files is the finding, and a per-file list leaves that comparison to the reader","Because verdicts cannot be graded","Because six verdicts take longer to compute","Because a verdict has no tolerance"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The useful output of a campaign is which files differ from the rest, and that is a statement about the set rather than about any member. A per-file list defers the comparison to the moment in the day when the reader is least likely to make it carefully.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'A campaign reports that 1 file needed a depth unit conversion. If one file''s unit string is unrecognised, in which direction does that count go wrong?', '["Too low, because no conversion is attempted and nobody records a decision","Too high, because an unrecognised unit is treated as foreign by default","Too high, because the file is counted once for each unrecognised curve","It cannot go wrong, since the pipeline sets the flag"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A file can come through unconverted without anyone deciding it should be left alone, and the count then reads lower than the truth. This is why you confirm that every file declared a unit you recognise before trusting a count of 1.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'For a count of 1 on the conversion field to be correct, what else must be true?', '["The other five files were not examined, since a campaign reports only exceptions","The conversion factor was applied to every curve in feet_20.las","The other five files declared a metric depth unit and were correctly left alone","feet_20.las was the largest file in the delivery"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The count is a statement about all six files, and confirming that a file needed no action is real work. A campaign that reports only its exceptions gives you no way to do that half of the check.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'Among the campaign''s fields, which one counts the files that conform rather than the files that differ?', '["Files needing a depth unit conversion, which returns 1","Files with a uniform depth step, which returns 5","Dead curves detected, which returns 1","Curves imported across the campaign, which returns 24"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Adjacent fields carry opposite polarity, and only the wording separates them. Carrying the exception habit across to the uniform field produces 1, which answers the failure question instead.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'Why is a pass count the more useful shape for a delivery note?', '["It is always the larger of the two numbers, which reads better in a summary table","It removes the need to name the exception","It counts the same population as the dead curve field, so the two can be compared directly","It states what downstream code may rely on and carries the batch size with it"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Five files carry a spacing that downstream code may assume and one does not, which is an instruction somebody can act on. A pass count also forces you to have tested every file, since you cannot claim five passed without testing five.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'A curve holds 1 finite sample in 201. How does the campaign treat it?', '["Dead, because a single finite sample in 201 cannot support any statistic that is worth computing or reporting","Dead, because it fails exactly the same test that the campaign''s one dead curve fails inside the nullheavy_20.las column","Not dead, because the test is exact and has no threshold, though it is unusable and belongs in your notes","Excluded from the curve count entirely"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Deadness means no finite samples at all, so one sample keeps the curve out of the graded count while leaving it useless in practice. Applying a threshold instead is the classic error and it produces a total that two people cannot agree on.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'Why does an exact definition of a dead curve matter more across a batch than on a single file?', '["Because an exact test gives two people the same total, while a judgement gives two totals and an argument","Because larger batches contain proportionally more dead curves","Because the campaign cannot open every file it counts","Because thresholds get harder to compute as the population grows larger"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A count with a fuzzy definition cannot be added up across files, since every reader sets the boundary somewhere different. The exactness is what lets the number travel.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'You know your campaign''s field list does not cover everything a file could get wrong. What is the right response?', '["Add every test you can think of, so that nothing at all escapes the report","Run the campaign once per format convention in the delivery","Restrict the delivery to files the campaign can fully describe","Say what the campaign does not cover when you hand the results over"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A campaign''s field list defines the exceptions it can see, and wrapped_12.las is ordinary on all three exception fields while differing from the others in format. Padding the report with tests nobody acts on makes it longer and less likely to be read.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'GR''s flagged samples sit at the top of its column. What does that pattern usually indicate?', '["The interval was spliced between two logging runs","The tool was coming on depth","The hole was washed out over that interval","The vendor removed readings it could not stand behind"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Where the gaps sit tells you what happened, and flags at the top of a column are the ordinary signature of a tool starting to record partway into the run. The pattern is visible in the file, so most of this question is answerable without asking anyone.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'Should nullheavy_20.las be held back from the interpreter while the neutron query is open?', '["No, because two of its curves have 0 nulls of 201 and the well is usable once you are precise about which part you have","Yes, until the neutron curve has been redelivered","Yes, because its null total is the largest in the delivery","No, because the campaign graded the file successfully"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A dead curve is a reason to be exact about what you have rather than to quarantine a well. RHOB in G/C3 and DT in US/M are complete and GR remains usable with its gaps recorded.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'Why can no amount of care with a total recover the parts it was summed from?', '["Because the parser discards the per curve counts as soon as it has summed them into the file total","Because a total is stored at a lower precision than the several parts that were added together to make it","Because the campaign grades only the total","Because addition is not reversible, so the distribution is gone once the parts become one number"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The same total is consistent with several different arrangements of the underlying data, and nothing in the number distinguishes them. That is why the composition has to be attached before the total leaves your desk.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'Which campaign number looks like a plain count and yet conceals a mechanism?', '["The 24 curves imported across the campaign","The 5 files with a uniform depth step","The 161 depth samples in wrapped_12.las","The 17 flagged nulls in basic_20.las"]'::jsonb, 2, 'wrapped_12.las is LAS 1.2 with wrap set to YES, and the route the parser takes to 161 is the subject of the next module. A count with no visible structure is not necessarily a count with no structure.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'Which sentence reports nullheavy_20.las''s null total in composed form?', '["272 flagged nulls and one dead curve in nullheavy_20.las","272 flagged samples across the 4 value curves of nullheavy_20.las at 201 samples each, of which 201 are NPHI in V/V with no finite samples and 71 are gaps in GR in GAPI","272 flagged nulls in nullheavy_20.las, the largest count in the delivery","272 flagged nulls spread across the file''s four value curves"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The composed version names the absent curve, separates it from the gaps, and shows that the dead curve sits inside the 272 rather than beside it. Reporting the total and the dead curve as two items is the version that reads as two separate problems of unstated size.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'Given only a total, what does a reader assume about its parts, and what does that assumption cost?', '["That the parts are unknown, so they will come back and ask you for the breakdown before acting","That one large part dominates the rest, which would happen to be a safe assumption on this file","That the total was estimated rather than counted, so they will go on to treat it as an approximation","That the parts are broadly similar in size, which sends the wrong query to the wrong person"]'::jsonb, 3, 'An even spread is what a single summary number suggests when nothing contradicts it, and here 201 of the 272 sit in one column. Acting on a picture of a generally gappy file leaves the largest item in it unaddressed.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'A field has found an exception in every delivery from one source and now finds none. What do you do?', '["Check the test, because a problem that stopped appearing and a check that stopped working look the same","Record the improvement in the delivery log and then move on to the next batch that arrives from that source","Widen the tolerance used by the test so that the field becomes more sensitive on the next delivery from that source","Treat it as clear evidence that the supplier has corrected their export settings since the last delivery"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Movement in a field is the signal, and it deserves the same scrutiny in both directions. A zero is only a finding once you know the check still runs.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'What does the engine gain by reshaping the data section from a flat token stream rather than from line boundaries?', '["It reads wrapped files faster than a wrap aware reader would.","There is one reader to test and fix, so a wrapped file is not a rarely exercised branch that quietly rots while the common path gets the attention.","It allows the curve count to be inferred from the data rather than the header.","It removes the need for a curve section."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The 161 samples from wrapped_12.las come from the same code that produced 301 from basic_20.las. One code path means one set of goldens exercising it.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'The LAS data section rule is that values appear in curve order, one full set per depth step, with no gaps. What kind of statement is that?', '["A statement about the sequence of values, which holds whether the writer used one line per record or several.","A statement about line layout, which is why a reader has to group lines before it can reshape the values into curves.","A statement about the header, which the data section then repeats.","A statement about units, which is why conversion happens after the reshape."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Reassembling from the sequence reads the guarantee the format gives. Reassembling from line boundaries reads a convention the format does not require, and then needs a special branch when the convention fails.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'A stray insertion in the middle of a data section happens to amount to a whole record''s worth of tokens. What does the parser do?', '["It stops with a ragged or truncated error, because any insertion changes the token count and the guard tests the token count against the curve count.","It parses successfully and the shift is confined to the record that was damaged.","It parses successfully, and every value after the insertion point sits one slot out of place.","It drops the stray tokens and continues."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The guard is a divisibility test, so an insertion that preserves divisibility passes it. The misplaced values are all real log readings, which is what makes this failure quiet.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'What is the divisibility guard, precisely?', '["A structural test that confirms the values are in curve order and that no record is missing from the middle of the file.","A necessary condition rather than a sufficient one.","A check of the reshaped sample count against the header depth frame.","A test that the file declares WRAP correctly."]'::jsonb, 1, 'It catches every corruption whose effect on the token count is not a multiple of the curve count, and none whose effect is. Believing it validates structure is the classic overreading of it.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'Which two reader checks catch damage that the parser''s own guard cannot see?', '["The curve kind assignment and the per curve unit conversion.","The wrap declaration and the null declaration.","The campaign curve total and the dead curve count.","The header depth frame cross-check, and the depth column itself through monotonicity and the uniform step test."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A record missing from the middle leaves a gap that a step test allowed to fail will find, and a one slot shift puts a curve value into the depth column so it stops being monotonic. Neither check was designed as a parser check.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'How does a null value behave in a wrapped file?', '["It occupies its slot in curve order like any other value, on whichever continuation line that slot falls.","It is written on a line of its own so that the wrap layout stays regular.","It ends the current record early, and the next line starts a new depth.","It is omitted, which is why wrapped files have fewer tokens."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Wrapping changes where the line breaks fall and changes nothing about the order or the meaning of the columns.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'Suppose the same well had been exported with 10 curves including depth, still in wrapped mode with one full record per depth step. Which counts change?', '["The sample count changes, because more curves means more samples in the same interval.","Only the line count changes, because tokens are counted per curve.","None of the three changes, because the depth frame is fixed.","The token count and the line count change, and the sample count of 161 does not."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The number of depth steps is a property of the depth frame rather than of how many curves were logged. Expecting the sample count to move with the curve count is the classic confusion here.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'Which multiplication checks the reshape of wrapped_12.las in the other direction?', '["161 samples times 3 lines per depth step gives 805 tokens, which is the same check run through the layout.","483 physical lines times the 5 declared curves gives the token count.","161 samples times 5 curves gives 805 tokens.","805 tokens multiplied by the 5 declared curves gives the sample count."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The three counts are three views of the same data section, so 161 times 5 returns the token count and 3 times 161 returns the 483 lines.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'In the campaign workflow, which step turns a count into something a project can act on?', '["The step that attaches a file name to each exception.","The step that runs every file through the unchanged import pipeline and produces one row per file.","The step that reads the six totals off the campaign panel.","The step that reconciles the readings which can be checked twice."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A project schedules a morning against a file name rather than against a number. Reconciliation and decomposition make a reading defensible, and the name is what makes it actionable.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'Scattered nulls run from 6 in feet_20.las to 17 in basic_20.las, and neither file is flagged as an exception. What does an unflagged file mean?', '["That the file was fully verified by the campaign and needs no further reading before it enters the registry.","That nothing in it crossed a threshold, which is not the same statement as the file being right.","That the file holds no nulls at all.","That the file was excluded from the aggregates."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every file in this delivery carries some nulls, which is normal. A campaign certifies nothing about the files it says nothing about.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'What does a campaign not do?', '["It does not run the same pipeline the tier below used, since aggregates need their own code path.","It does not report per file rows at all, since a campaign reports only the totals.","It does not decide, and it does not replace opening the file it points at.","It does not read the header of any file, since the aggregates come from the data sections."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Once the campaign has named nullheavy_20.las, you open nullheavy_20.las. The campaign chose the file rather than doing the work, and whether the dead curve is flagged, dropped or queried is a decision a person writes down.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'Which of the six campaign readings has no exception in it, and what does that tell you?', '["The dead curve count, because a count of 1 means the delivery is uniform.","The curve total, because every file contributes 4, so the reading describes the format of the delivery rather than any file in it.","The wrapped sample count, because only one file is wrapped.","The null count, because every file carries nulls."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A reading shared by every file tells you about the format, and a reading that differs tells you about a file. Recognising the first kind saves you from staring at it.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'Which consumer of the registry depends most directly on the per curve unit work rather than on the depth column?', '["Mapping, because a gridded surface inherits the unit of every curve that was used in building it, including the log curves it never reads.","Well correlation, because tops are picked on curve values rather than on the depth column that carries them.","Volumetrics, because a resource number is computed from curve units.","Petrophysics, because a sonic in the wrong unit returns a porosity that is wrong and plausible at once."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correlation and mapping depend on the depth column, where one well left in feet plants a false structural feature. None of them re-checks the units, which is the point of doing it once.'),
  ('welldata', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'What is the dataset and the shape of the Expert capstone paper?', '["All six teaching LAS files, with six campaign level fields read from one panel and no file switching.","One file at a time, with five fields read on feet_20.las and one read on irregular_20.las after switching files in the panel.","All six files, with each of the six graded fields read on a different one of the six teaching files in turn.","The wrapped file alone, since it carries the only graded sample count."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Reading five fields on one file and switching once for the sixth is the shape of the Professional paper at the tier below. Every Expert field is a campaign level number.');
